          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why do some categories need a fourth or fifth character while others do not?",
          "answer": "Extra characters add detail that the shorter code cannot hold. The passages describe a few reasons:\n\n- **Clinical detail:** The US version pushes severity, acuity, laterality (right, left, bilateral) and complications into the code itself. Heart failure (I50) has a mechanism axis and an acuity axis, so its codes need more characters (coding-heart-failure-i50, coding-icd10-cm-versus-who-icd10).\n- **Episode of care:** Injuries, and some obstetric categories, use a seventh character to record whether the encounter is initial, subsequent, or a sequela (coding-icd10-cm-versus-who-icd10).\n- **Complication status:** The fourth character of COPD (J44) records whether a complication such as an infection or exacerbation is present at this encounter (coding-copd-j44).\n\nSome codes are complete at three characters. Essential hypertension (I10) is one, because the category has no further subdivisions to record (coding-hypertension-i10). Where a category needs a longer code but an intervening position is empty, the placeholder \"X\" holds that position so the final character lands in the right place (coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions).\n\nThe retrieved passages describe what the extra characters record for these examples. They do not give a full rationale for every category, so I can't say why each individual category has the length it does. The coding manual or a trained coder would be the place to check a specific code.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
